      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the insight. when you talk about using proxies like the percentage of local institutions or small businesses, how do we distinguish between a \"high-risk\" neighborhood that's just transient vs a \"low-risk\" neighborhood that has more roots? like, if an area has a lot of turnover or different types of community hubs, does that statistically correlate with higher default rates? i'm trying to figure out how to weight those localized vibes into a hard math model.",
