      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. but when we talk about \"social cohesion\" or \"permanence,\" isn't there a risk that the model just ends up characterizing any diverse or rapidly changing urban area as a high-risk zone? if we use tenure and established business count, wouldn't that basically just tag any developing or multicultural neighborhood as a \"low cohesion\" environment? i'm wondering if there's a way to build this out so we aren't basically just penalizing areas that don't look like traditional, homogenous suburbs.",
